#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231269 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/id #uuid "7ad46566-b122-5de3-9b87-9beec9ac6761" :question/math-academy-id "e-21877" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.25\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t) = 3\\sin(6t)\\,\\textrm{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nmy'' = -ky + F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.25y'' = - ky + 3\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\ny'' + 4ky = 12\\sin(6t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution $y_c$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos \\omega t + B\\sin \\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega$ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 4k\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nk = \\dfrac14\\omega^2.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=12\\sin(6t),$ which is $6,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac14\\omega^2 = \\dfrac14\\cdot6^2 = 9 \\,\\textrm{kg/s}^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231270 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "f41473ff-05a6-5f3e-ae5b-0584810aa0dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-21878" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t) = 4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y = R\\cos(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n- $y = R\\sin(4t+\\phi) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n- $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $\\phi$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega.$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=4\\cos(4t)+12\\sin(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega = 4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)}$\n\n- $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi)} = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)}$\n\n- $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t} = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega$ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p(t)=t(C\\cos(4t)+D\\sin(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega=4$ into the differential equation, $y'' + \\omega^2y = f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8(D-2Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8(C+2Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 8D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-8C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-\\dfrac{3}{2}$ and $D=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(t) = -\\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y = R\\cos{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n- $y = R\\sin{(4t + \\phi)} - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\n- $y = A\\cos(4t) + B\\sin(4t) - \\dfrac{3}{2}t\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{1}{2}t\\sin(4t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231380 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38826ba-56b1-59ff-8262-fda4fdc74589" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Parameter that Results in Resonance"}] :question/id #uuid "bb405211-5b18-5cc1-a264-bdf6e2fbcc60" :question/math-academy-id "e-6735" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 25y =2\\sin^2{pt},\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the general solution of the differential equation. The complementary solution $y_c$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 25\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\omega = 5,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos{5 t} + B\\sin{5 t}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $2 \\sin^2 pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega = 5.$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2 \\sin^2 pt = 1 - \\cos 2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$ Therefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when\n\n$$\n2p = 5 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad p = \\dfrac{5}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7a6da11-59bd-53ee-a9ad-f2ddcff4c315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380797} {:answer/id #uuid "823e648e-c330-5318-acce-790985cf1ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7a6da11-59bd-53ee-a9ad-f2ddcff4c315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380797} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f1f833-70f9-57ff-a5a2-3b166b487bf9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f6ac78e-cb74-5768-92ef-290d5885de7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343459" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),$ which is $5,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38826ba-56b1-59ff-8262-fda4fdc74589" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Parameter that Results in Resonance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2880552-09e4-5c2c-82ac-e9df538b2bce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380801} {:answer/id #uuid "f057523a-d238-5076-8632-68055ef85b98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380802} {:answer/id #uuid "e4821695-3a24-52c6-ad0c-603805fae423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380803} {:answer/id #uuid "3383cee3-75d4-5538-97bd-0ef77ea7e48b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380804} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0adaa9-121a-5a2f-90e7-9f1ba651f0e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb0adaa9-121a-5a2f-90e7-9f1ba651f0e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380805} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae8d4203-5b05-5a93-bc54-23c28053f48b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f9b7e48-4089-50c8-a31d-fcc6165e603c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343662" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=1\\,⟹\\,\\omega =1.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =1.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c23c651d-0acd-57c4-8545-0d8e901058cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c23c651d-0acd-57c4-8545-0d8e901058cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380808} :answer-field/id #uuid "def07b86-c2d2-5c0b-97bc-3d4d516c1354" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f380b02b-b59b-5ba3-823b-21abb72505ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-343667" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.6\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.6{y}^{″}=-ky-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{3}y=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{3}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{3}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),$ which is $5,$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{3}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot {5}^{2}=15\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f2a028-8f26-59e4-980a-07753fa12d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23f2a028-8f26-59e4-980a-07753fa12d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380811} :answer-field/id #uuid "722899fe-552d-5df2-a184-60487a204df9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d35c95d-0ded-5b1c-a0b9-f8820ef48108" :question/math-academy-id "q-343679" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=2cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.8{y}^{″}=-ky+2cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{4}y=\\frac{5}{2}cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{4}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\frac{5}{2}cos\\,(\\frac{5}{2}t),$ which is $\\frac{5}{2},$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}=5\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc41aa19-0ad5-58c6-8e54-3335a4eccb99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc41aa19-0ad5-58c6-8e54-3335a4eccb99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380814} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cb4b9c6-aefa-5b3f-8689-c3aa57c9961b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1708a22-9a02-56ee-a9df-bebff83b7dba" :question/math-academy-id "q-343691" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-2)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-2).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{18}{5}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2454e747-bfcd-5336-8dee-2dbbf5a40c37" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Spring Constant Given an External Periodic Force"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da74498-207a-5cd4-a861-2c26491640bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380817} {:answer/id #uuid "bffc7f9f-08d4-5413-bf59-1531d65e251e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1da74498-207a-5cd4-a861-2c26491640bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380817} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa68dacb-a747-523d-bcbc-85d4a281848f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b0eca4d-00c5-51c3-8921-eb16a9fcdf6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343699" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $4.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {4}^{2}=8\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ae5dab5-06a2-5869-852c-d9ef68b2e33a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380821} {:answer/id #uuid "b7026065-3548-50fa-bbed-7e5c56ae972e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380822} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf01c0d-6d5d-57d2-b15c-8a7bf5c874f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380823} {:answer/id #uuid "9c008c9d-cab2-5ab5-a86a-4ceb00f3f99e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380824} {:answer/id #uuid "48eceee6-00ad-5e9a-b93f-ef6704cee409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48eceee6-00ad-5e9a-b93f-ef6704cee409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380825} :answer-field/id #uuid "2302b378-5802-5058-bcf9-30aa2205b2a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9da4341a-f191-500a-9470-7d945f225a70" :question/math-academy-id "q-343746" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[16(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 16D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{8}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nOnly solution II matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b167eff-cf65-5562-8a27-8b808616d743" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380828} {:answer/id #uuid "b4357829-79fc-5581-ab6c-b9873f357c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380829} {:answer/id #uuid "04d57c3e-b151-5fea-abe3-04258ad3372b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380830} {:answer/id #uuid "bc24f523-76cb-5ae2-b1d6-253a37aadaa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380831} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa1faed-00ce-50ff-b3a0-0c392430b6c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc24f523-76cb-5ae2-b1d6-253a37aadaa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380831} :answer-field/id #uuid "66468e49-8da9-54cd-9ea2-0083e0b7f591" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "afac3968-7d7a-5ba6-b9cb-6d93eb3f2185" :question/math-academy-id "q-343756" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f17327b-1293-5d02-9614-56cb5bc32a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369135} {:answer/id #uuid "1632a717-fa9b-5a41-b58e-6cbba7f3e9bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369136} {:answer/id #uuid "5be7ba38-0784-532d-823b-79721c845b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369137} {:answer/id #uuid "30dcb20d-e5bc-50aa-a16d-c0cb4a88e0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369138} {:answer/id #uuid "dca16548-7f00-5f60-a5f4-041400b2a11a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f17327b-1293-5d02-9614-56cb5bc32a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369135} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7490e5a-e807-53a6-b133-4feed29aa08b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "512495a2-8bfb-52b3-bf2e-01e8a787e6fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-343761" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $D=-2.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380833 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0124c94-a94c-5ec8-a046-066cabfa9fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380835} {:answer/id #uuid "223bc31e-abf8-5a33-914f-16d1fa75ea6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380836} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd9ab2e-bb15-592d-b984-04e14f27c711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380837} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a41f93-299d-5504-82c8-7ad8311f0e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380838} {:answer/id #uuid "9557d516-cbf9-59b8-941c-027e4cc97250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0124c94-a94c-5ec8-a046-066cabfa9fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380835} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d2873ce-bb72-573f-a5f4-0eeabb5b3b71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2da542b9-0ae7-57d9-b677-3d305e8a1d6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343763" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $5.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =5.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =5$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(10D-25Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-(10C+25Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)]+25t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ 10D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-10C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{4}{5}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380840 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b181416-e2e0-5ded-ac39-866f8cb72cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380842} {:answer/id #uuid "93e09bd3-60f7-5ae4-8001-31879edbcef5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380843} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b098b1-2c3c-53f4-aa4a-72b5c9b0b36b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380844} {:answer/id #uuid "dd415870-e1fd-51e0-b293-614354e67c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380845} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1ea5a5-f8e4-55b0-8f30-6b81552147d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b181416-e2e0-5ded-ac39-866f8cb72cb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380842} :answer-field/id #uuid "57ffe78d-4785-5eb4-a03d-282ce87f89f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49ae5320-1d28-50cb-bba8-dbc684113b47" :question/math-academy-id "q-343764" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{3}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38826ba-56b1-59ff-8262-fda4fdc74589" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Parameter that Results in Resonance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cb8d6bd-dfd6-5028-9d6d-1c57a5d744a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380849} {:answer/id #uuid "c033e580-a027-589e-9c75-96d85c9150d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380850} {:answer/id #uuid "e404d055-670f-5f35-9dd1-912664f7ab0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380851} {:answer/id #uuid "f78b4282-df9b-542e-a8bb-72ed8009d6f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380852} {:answer/id #uuid "2043f085-f32a-57da-8dfa-a8b8f6670764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c033e580-a027-589e-9c75-96d85c9150d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380850} :answer-field/id #uuid "b52f5944-1916-5ebc-b45a-e37fd152cdf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb390a83-e4ea-5b09-a76c-3676fed5e3fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-64647" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =3.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38826ba-56b1-59ff-8262-fda4fdc74589" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Parameter that Results in Resonance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91716b9b-25b8-5f7d-af82-c5c6244ffece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231216} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d47177-e7eb-5bbe-ad61-37d6ebe28dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231217} {:answer/id #uuid "f093da8e-4b94-5386-8861-cef36519b4aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231218} {:answer/id #uuid "2456a120-3251-59f3-b369-f5d67663ae79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231219} {:answer/id #uuid "532c87ef-52c1-5250-9bf9-3d5e673bd018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2456a120-3251-59f3-b369-f5d67663ae79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231219} :answer-field/id #uuid "1232421e-2c06-5494-9d63-831097f76c15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6af51c6-8a3b-5e30-952e-168721f14820" :question/math-academy-id "q-64650" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =4.$ Since \n\n$$\nf(t)=2{cos}^{2}⁡pt=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2pt,\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $2p.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\frac{\\omega }{2}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38826ba-56b1-59ff-8262-fda4fdc74589" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Parameter that Results in Resonance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e5f052c-68fc-5321-80cb-44f6cb6af302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380856} {:answer/id #uuid "e886383b-ffee-592e-8b0a-e8a7459f7c35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380857} {:answer/id #uuid "9b0a9a46-a11e-5c80-b0d7-9aac3c1dbb99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380858} {:answer/id #uuid "1026ae64-c001-5cc7-a581-f1b073fea399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380859} {:answer/id #uuid "f93b9991-1dd8-5c6d-9f6b-521444e9865f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1026ae64-c001-5cc7-a581-f1b073fea399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380859} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d2c6bf2-0e7e-52eb-a359-545f9f608d70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3dda6cd8-1859-51bd-a910-653c14acd8c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-94235" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+7y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=7\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{7}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{7}t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\sqrt{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f38826ba-56b1-59ff-8262-fda4fdc74589" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Value of a Parameter that Results in Resonance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4150d82-23d6-57c8-a6ba-8ce1943ecd98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380863} {:answer/id #uuid "1589c358-904d-509e-a205-df11f2ad90d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380864} {:answer/id #uuid "cab0a2f7-82c9-59ca-ab20-7d1f2d0ea3da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380865} {:answer/id #uuid "f827e59d-31ce-5a31-a5d6-b1727d2d43fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380866} {:answer/id #uuid "4dffa9f1-724f-569d-ab2b-6ffd5b0fb65f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cab0a2f7-82c9-59ca-ab20-7d1f2d0ea3da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380865} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aadd5f3-5b93-540c-926a-638711a144b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ad1d4d8-5acf-555d-8d65-d8a0dd8159f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94238" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt+\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=6\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{6}t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{6}t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt+\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=\\sqrt{6}.$"}]}
